WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.060
(TV static)

00:00:01.060 --> 00:00:04.310
(Netflix logo droning)

00:00:08.410 --> 00:00:11.346
- Wait is Dale Winton the one
who did Supermarket Sweep?

00:00:11.346 --> 00:00:12.179
Ugh.

00:00:12.179 --> 00:00:13.570
When I was in (mumbles)
me and my housemates

00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:15.310
used to play Supermarket Sweep.

00:00:15.310 --> 00:00:17.017
The security guard at
Tesco's would be like,

00:00:17.017 --> 00:00:19.267
"You need to stop,
because this is too much."

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:23.320
My name's Mason.

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:25.633
I grew up on the border of Suri.

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:27.424
I grew up in a village that's
super duper tiny called

00:00:27.424 --> 00:00:30.470
(mumbles).

00:00:30.470 --> 00:00:31.733
Two trains an hour.

00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:35.430
So now I live in

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:37.853
a place (laughs) I can't
pronounce, Llanelli.

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:41.660
Every single Welsh person's
gonna kill me for that.

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:44.910
I truly love Wales, it's
so much fun to be here.

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:51.310
I think the first time I
maybe even thought I was gay

00:00:51.310 --> 00:00:52.690
I think it was Dylan.

00:00:52.690 --> 00:00:54.910
Dylan was my sexual awakening.

00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:56.233
Dylan from Casualty.

00:00:57.270 --> 00:00:59.900
I must have been about eight years old.

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:01.160
That's Dylan.

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:04.623
That beautiful, blonde, absolute stunner.

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:08.840
I think he died in the
end, I think he was shot.

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:10.490
And I didn't watch it ever again.

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:15.000
So I came out to Mum,
I was 18 at the time.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.050
I went home and I said,
"Mum, let's have a chat."

00:01:18.050 --> 00:01:21.040
And she said to me, "Do I have
to turn the TV off for this?"

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:22.560
I said, "Yeah, yeah, turn it off."

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:24.950
And she just went to
me, "Do you like boys?"

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:28.430
Yeah, and she was like,
"Fantastic, are you in for dinner?"

00:01:28.430 --> 00:01:29.673
That was it.

00:01:29.673 --> 00:01:32.680
It was like short conversation.

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:35.523
I fully expected some sort of party.

00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:38.890
I wanted balloons, everything.

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:42.260
There's an episode of Ugly
Betty and Justin comes out.

00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:44.062
They fully give him a pinata,

00:01:44.062 --> 00:01:45.342
there's rainbows and everything,

00:01:45.342 --> 00:01:46.830
and I was like, "That's
what coming out is,"

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:48.783
and I got absolutely none of it.

00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:51.160
Christian from Eastenders,

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:53.710
that was a different kind
of vibe for me though,

00:01:53.710 --> 00:01:56.650
because he was a fair
amount older than me,

00:01:56.650 --> 00:01:59.780
secondly he's got huge
muscles, tiny head though.

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:01.370
He was just so masculine.

00:02:01.370 --> 00:02:05.590
It made me challenge my
own stereotypes of gay.

00:02:05.590 --> 00:02:07.270
When you're younger you're drawn to people

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:11.410
because you look for guidance in society.

00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:12.860
You are given cues.

00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:16.900
Now I'm 24, I look more
towards queer media

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:19.700
than I do towards straight
media for those kind of cues.

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:23.670
When I was younger
Facebook wasn't a thing.

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:25.470
It was Bebo.

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:30.050
The greatest social media
network of all time.

00:02:30.050 --> 00:02:33.180
It was there that I first saw
anything from Queer as Folk.

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:34.970
Was like, "Ah, gay people?

00:02:34.970 --> 00:02:36.490
But this is TV!"

00:02:36.490 --> 00:02:40.090
It was a real chance to
learn about gay culture,

00:02:40.090 --> 00:02:41.300
'cause I had never been to a gay bar

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:42.900
before my first year of uni.

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:45.660
And it fully thrusts you into that world.

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:48.210
Representation is one of
the biggest driving forces

00:02:48.210 --> 00:02:51.200
into more people being
accepting and educating,

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:53.033
and TV is the best way to do that.

00:02:54.210 --> 00:02:56.790
I truly believe RuPaul's Drag Race

00:02:56.790 --> 00:02:59.610
is the most diverse representation on TV

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:00.850
that you can find.

00:03:00.850 --> 00:03:03.850
Queer men, the occasional
trans person, black people,

00:03:03.850 --> 00:03:06.690
white people, Latinos, people
who speak different languages,

00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:08.770
and the whole thing is a message of love.

00:03:08.770 --> 00:03:11.400
Also you can follow the
journey, it doesn't end.

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:12.233
That's the best part I think,

00:03:12.233 --> 00:03:14.470
because the show ends and
you just get to follow them.

00:03:14.470 --> 00:03:17.410
Like Trixie and Katya, I loved them

00:03:17.410 --> 00:03:18.680
and now they've got their own show

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:20.430
and I can watch all the time.

00:03:20.430 --> 00:03:23.430
Sex Education is really
good, and it's in Wales.

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:26.010
You see the relationship between a gay man

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:27.070
and a straight boy,

00:03:27.070 --> 00:03:28.160
but they're best friends.

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:29.980
When I was younger more so,

00:03:29.980 --> 00:03:32.020
it was always like the thing, "Ew, no,

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:33.720
you're gonna try and sleep with me."

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:36.147
And I'm like, "No."

00:03:36.147 --> 00:03:38.690
"You may be a boy,that doesn't
mean I wanna sleep with you."

00:03:38.690 --> 00:03:41.220
Seeing representation like that on TV

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:46.220
it allows for straight guys to
not receive as much weirdness

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:47.823
from their other friends.

00:03:48.820 --> 00:03:52.230
For me, as a mixed race gay man,

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:54.890
it's difficult to find a protagonist.

00:03:54.890 --> 00:03:56.390
Which is weird because I mean,

00:03:56.390 --> 00:03:58.900
I'm pretty sure there's quite a few of us.

00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:02.120
The closest I get to be
like, "Oh, we're the same,"

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:03.270
Is Justin from Ugly Betty.

00:04:03.270 --> 00:04:05.840
'Cause we were around a similar age,

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:08.510
he likes magazine, I like magazines.

00:04:08.510 --> 00:04:09.960
He's gay, I'm gay.

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:13.280
The only difference is that
he got a pinata and I didn't.

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:15.480
Like that's pretty much
the only difference.

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:19.430
When Pose came out, I was
like, "Oh, this is great!"

00:04:19.430 --> 00:04:21.520
So many black people,
so many people of color,

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:23.460
everyone's gay, everyone's trans.

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:26.060
It's just so good.

00:04:26.060 --> 00:04:27.820
And also it's like a great TV show.

00:04:27.820 --> 00:04:29.330
Which I think is important,

00:04:29.330 --> 00:04:30.670
because you can have a whole load

00:04:30.670 --> 00:04:32.370
of gay people make a TV show,

00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:33.950
but if it's not any good
people aren't gonna watch it,

00:04:33.950 --> 00:04:35.650
people aren't gonna pay attention.

00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:37.640
The more people that watch,

00:04:37.640 --> 00:04:38.820
the more people that are educated,

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:40.390
the more people that learn.

00:04:40.390 --> 00:04:42.600
And everyone's really
attractive on that show.

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:45.403
Like weirdly attractive,
it's somewhat annoying.

00:04:46.362 --> 00:04:50.195
(uplifting orchestral music)

